MANUAL SCHEMA CREATION

I haven't figured out how to tell Django that these exist, so for now we
have to do manual migrations to create new schema.  To do this, in /django/desidb:

  python manage.py makemigrations everest --empty

replacing everest with the appropriate django ap.  That will create a
file in everest/migrations.  Edit that file to have:

    operations = [
       migrations.RunSQL(
         sql = ( f"DO $do$ "
                 f"BEGIN "
                 f"  IF NOT EXISTS ( "
                 f"     SELECT FROM pg_catalog.pg_roles WHERE rolname='desi' "
                 f"  ) THEN "
                 f"     CREATE ROLE desi PASSWORD '{os.getenv('DESIPASSWORD')}' LOGIN; "
                 f"  END IF; "
                 f"END "
                 f"$do$;" ),
         reverse_sql = ""
       )
       migrations.RunSQL( 
         sql = 'CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS everest',
         reverse_sql = 'DROP SCHEMA IF EXISTS everest'
       ),
       migrations.RunSQL(
         sql = 'GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA everest TO desi',
         reverse_sql = 'REVOKE USAGE ON SCHEMA everest FROM desi'
       )
       migrations.RunSQL(
         sql = ( 'ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA everest '
                 'GRANT SELECT ON TABLES TO desi' )
         reverse_sql = ( 'ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA everest '
                         'REVOKE SELECT ON TABLES FROM desi' )
      )
    ]

or whatever SQL you really need.  (Remember to add 'import os' at the
top.)  (For schema creation, only include the user creation in the *very*
first one.  Change everest, of course, to whatever the schema is.)


======================================================================

NEW DATA RELEASE

* Add a django ap for the release; on the desidb spin instance, run:

    python manage.py startapp <releasename>

* Copy and properly edit (proofread several times to get everything, or
  you will muck up an older release!) the code from another release:

    desidb/<releasename>/models.py
    desidb/<releasename>/management/commands/load<release>.py

* Edit desidb/desidb/settings.py and add the new application to
  INSTALLED_APPS; follow the pattern of the previously existing releases.

* Create an "initial" manual database migration; this is necessary to
  create the postgresql schema, something that django doesn't natively
  know about.

    python manage.py makemigrations iron --name initial --empty

  Edit that file and copy the text from the 0001_initial.py of another
  release, replacing the release name as appropriate.  (Again, be
  careful and proofread multiple times to avoid corrupting something
  else.  If I'm not mistaken, the release name shows up 6 times.)

* Apply the initial database migration

    python manage.py migrate

* Make the database migration that creates the table for the new release

    python manage.py makemigrations <release>

  (Ideally, you can leave off the <release> at the end, as the only app
  with changes will be the new release you're making.)

* Apply the migrations

    python manage.py migrate

* Load the release.  IMPORTANT: run this in a tmux session.  Otherwise,
  the spin shell timeout may cancel your load before it finishes.

    nohup python manage.py load<release> -n 19900101 -v 3 2>&1 | tee
    load<release>.out
    
  This takes a long time (more than a day).

======================================================================

USERS

There aren't built-in schema files for creating the users.  I did this
manually.

  python manage.py createsuperuser --username=admin --email=raknop@lbl.gov
  
I then manually added the "desi" user with the standard desi password.
